     1  # Experiment 006 — Timer Overhead Bias Quantification and Correction
     2  
     3  **Date**: 2025-10-14
     4  **Status**: Complete
     5  
     6  ## Hypothesis (from experiment_005)
     7  
     8  The timeit-measured per-call time follows the model:
     9  
    10      measured_time(N) = true_time + overhead/N
    11  
    12  where `overhead` is the fixed timeit overhead per repeat. By measuring at
    13  multiple N values and fitting this model, we can estimate the true time
    14  (overhead→0) and the overhead itself.
    15  
    16  This is a linear model in 1/N:
    17  
    18      measured_time = true_time + overhead × (1/N)
    19  
    20  We fit this model to experiment_005 data and extrapolate to N=∞ (1/N=0)
    21  to get corrected times. We then compare corrected times with experiment_001
    22  (perf_counter) measurements.
    23  
    24  ## Design
    25  
    26  - Uses experiment_005 data (2520 rows, already collected)
    27  - Fits linear model: time = a + b*(1/N) via least squares
    28  - Extrapolates to N=∞ for corrected times
    29  - Compares corrected times with experiment_001 perf_counter results
    30  - Validates model by checking R² and residual patterns
    31  - Python standard library only
    32  
    33  ## Results
    34  
    35  ### Model Fit Quality
    36  
    37  | Metric | Value |
    38  |--------|-------|
    39  | R² > 0.95 | 12/24 conditions (50%) |
    40  | R² < 0.95 | 12/24 conditions (50%) |
    41  | R² mean | 0.7434 |
    42  
    43  **Conditions with poor fit (R² < 0.90):**
    44  - All insertion_sort conditions (11/12) — R² ranges 0.03–0.69
    45  - builtin_sorted at n=128 duplicates (R²=0.84)
    46  - builtin_sorted at n=512 duplicates (R²=0.53)
    47  
    48  **Why insertion_sort fits poorly:** The overhead/N component is negligible
    49  compared to the true time (insertion_sort takes 1–5000 μs vs overhead ~0.1 μs).
    50  With such a small signal-to-noise ratio for the overhead term, the linear
    51  relationship with 1/N is dominated by random noise, yielding low R².
    52  
    53  **Why builtin_sorted fits well:** For fast operations, the overhead is a
    54  significant fraction of the measured time (e.g., at N=1, overhead is ~20% of
    55  total time for n=32). This creates a clear linear trend with 1/N.
    56  
    57  ### Overhead Estimates
    58  
    59  | Algorithm | Overhead Range | Notes |
    60  |-----------|---------------|-------|
    61  | builtin_sorted | +0.089 to +1.818 μs | All positive, as expected |
    62  | insertion_sort | -37.156 to +0.077 μs | Mostly negative (model doesn't apply) |
    63  
    64  Negative overhead for insertion_sort confirms the model is inappropriate —
    65  the true time dominates so completely that the regression line can tilt
    66  slightly negative due to noise.
    67  
    68  ### Comparison: Corrected timeit vs experiment_001 (perf_counter)
    69  
    70  | Size | Input | Algorithm | Corrected | Exp001 | Diff% |
    71  |------|-------|-----------|-----------|--------|-------|
    72  | 32 | random | builtin_sorted | 0.407 | 0.368 | +10.5% |
    73  | 32 | sorted | builtin_sorted | 0.268 | 0.240 | +11.6% |
    74  | 32 | reverse | builtin_sorted | 0.286 | 0.261 | +9.6% |
    75  | 32 | duplicates | builtin_sorted | 0.345 | 0.325 | +6.1% |
    76  | 128 | random | builtin_sorted | 1.991 | 1.913 | +4.1% |
    77  | 128 | sorted | builtin_sorted | 0.778 | 0.555 | +40.2% |
    78  | 128 | reverse | builtin_sorted | 0.896 | 0.695 | +28.9% |
