   105  | 128 | reverse | 337.6x | 430.9x | -21.6% |
   106  | 512 | random | 267.6x | 278.6x | -3.9% |
   107  | 512 | sorted | 11.3x | 15.3x | -25.8% |
   108  | 512 | reverse | 1643.7x | 2234.6x | -26.4% |
   109  
   110  **Key finding:** Ratios from corrected timeit data are consistently lower
   111  than experiment_001 ratios (by 4–26%). This means:
   112  
   113  1. **Experiment_001 ratios are slightly inflated** — perf_counter underestimates
   114     builtin_sorted times more than it underestimates insertion_sort times (because
   115     insertion_sort is already slow enough that timer resolution doesn't matter).
   116  2. **The inflation is largest for presorted inputs** — Timsort is fastest on
   117     sorted data, so timer resolution has the biggest impact on the denominator.
   118  3. **Qualitative conclusions unchanged** — insertion_sort is still orders of
   119     magnitude slower than builtin_sorted for all conditions.
   120  
   121  ### Residual Analysis (sample)
   122  
   123  For builtin_sorted at n=32, random:
   124  ```
   125  N=    1: measured= 0.514 μs  predicted= 0.514 μs  residual= +0.000 μs
   126  N=   10: measured= 0.416 μs  predicted= 0.418 μs  residual= -0.002 μs
   127  N=  100: measured= 0.409 μs  predicted= 0.408 μs  residual= +0.001 μs
   128  N= 1000: measured= 0.407 μs  predicted= 0.407 μs  residual= -0.000 μs
   129  ```
   130  Residuals are tiny (< 0.005 μs), confirming excellent model fit.
   131  
   132  For insertion_sort at n=512, random:
   133  ```
   134  N=    1: measured=2779.636 μs  predicted=2779.598 μs  residual= +0.038 μs
   135  N=   10: measured=2786.942 μs  predicted=2787.360 μs  residual= -0.418 μs
   136  N=  100: measured=2788.517 μs  predicted=2788.137 μs  residual= +0.380 μs
   137  ```
   138  Residuals are larger in absolute terms but tiny relative to the measured time
   139  (< 0.02%), confirming the overhead is negligible.
   140  
   141  ## Conclusions
   142  
   143  1. **The timer overhead model works well for fast operations** (builtin_sorted)
   144     but poorly for slow ones (insertion_sort). This is expected: when the true
   145     time dominates the overhead, the 1/N signal is lost in noise.
   146  
   147  2. **Corrected timeit times agree with perf_counter within 10%** for most
   148     conditions, validating both measurement approaches.
   149  
   150  3. **Large discrepancies (29–40%) for presorted inputs at larger sizes**
   151     suggest systematic differences between the two methods that warrant
   152     further investigation. Possible causes:
   153     - Timsort's adaptive behavior interacting differently with loop overhead
   154     - GC/memory allocation timing differences
   155     - OS scheduling effects during longer measurements
   156  
   157  4. **Experiment_001 ratios are slightly inflated** (by 4–26%) due to
   158     perf_counter underestimating the fast builtin_sorted times. The inflation
   159     is largest for presorted inputs where builtin_sorted is fastest.
   160  
   161  5. **Practical guidance:** For operations >10 μs, timer resolution is not
   162     a concern. For sub-microsecond operations, timeit with N≥100 gives
   163     reliable per-call estimates.
   164  
   165  ## Files
   166  
   167  - `experiment_006/benchmark_timer_bias.py` — Timer bias analysis script
   168  - `experiment_006/verify_timer_bias.py` — Verification script
   169  - `experiment_006/corrected_times.csv` — Corrected times (24 rows)
   170  - `experiment_006/report.md` — This report
   171  
   172  ## Limitations
   173  
   174  - Single platform (Python 3.13.15 / aarch64 Linux)
   175  - Only integer inputs tested
   176  - Model assumes constant overhead, which may not hold across all N values
   177  - 4 N values (1, 10, 100, 1000) may not be sufficient for robust fitting
   178  
   179  ## Reproducibility
   180  
   181  ```bash
   182  cd /workspace/experiment_006
   183  python3 benchmark_timer_bias.py
   184  python3 verify_timer_bias.py corrected_times.csv
   185  ```


